# Auth.js v5 (NextAuth) Standards

## Package Names

Auth.js v5 is published as `next-auth@5.x` for Next.js. The core is `@auth/core`. The D1 adapter is `@auth/d1-adapter`. Never use `next-auth@4.x` patterns (e.g., `getServerSession`, `getToken` from `next-auth/jwt` at the top level, `[...nextauth].ts` pages router).

```bash
npm install next-auth@5 @auth/d1-adapter
```

## Split Configuration Pattern (Edge-Safe)

Split auth config into two files to keep the edge-compatible middleware fast and avoid importing database adapters into edge bundles:

### `auth.config.ts` — Edge-Safe (No DB Adapter)

This file runs in edge middleware. It must NOT import database adapters, ORMs, or Node.js-only modules:

```ts
// src/auth.config.ts
import type { NextAuthConfig } from "next-auth";
import Google from "next-auth/providers/google";
import GitHub from "next-auth/providers/github";

export const authConfig = {
  providers: [
    Google({
      clientId: process.env.AUTH_GOOGLE_ID!,
      clientSecret: process.env.AUTH_GOOGLE_SECRET!,
    }),
    GitHub({
      clientId: process.env.AUTH_GITHUB_ID!,
      clientSecret: process.env.AUTH_GITHUB_SECRET!,
    }),
  ],
  pages: {
    signIn: "/login",
    error: "/login",
  },
  callbacks: {
    authorized({ auth, request: { nextUrl } }) {
      const isLoggedIn = !!auth?.user;
      const isProtected = nextUrl.pathname.startsWith("/dashboard");
      if (isProtected && !isLoggedIn) {
        return Response.redirect(new URL("/login", nextUrl));
      }
      return true;
    },
  },
} satisfies NextAuthConfig;
```

### `auth.ts` — Full Config (With DB Adapter)

This file adds the database adapter and exports the `auth()` function, handlers, and sign-in/sign-out methods. It runs in the Node.js runtime:

```ts
// src/auth.ts
import NextAuth from "next-auth";
import { authConfig } from "./auth.config";
import { D1Adapter } from "@auth/d1-adapter";
import { getCloudflareContext } from "@opennextjs/cloudflare";

async function getAuthConfig() {
  const { env } = await getCloudflareContext({ async: true });
  return {
    ...authConfig,
    adapter: D1Adapter(env.DB),
    session: { strategy: "jwt" as const },
    trustHost: true,
    secret: env.AUTH_SECRET,
    callbacks: {
      ...authConfig.callbacks,
      async jwt({ token, user }) {
        if (user) {
          token.id = user.id;
          token.role = user.role;
        }
        return token;
      },
      async session({ session, token }) {
        if (token) {
          session.user.id = token.id as string;
          session.user.role = token.role as string;
        }
        return session;
      },
    },
  };
}

const authResult = async () => NextAuth(await getAuthConfig());

export const handlers = async () => (await authResult()).handlers;
export const auth = async () => (await authResult()).auth();
export const signIn = async () => (await authResult()).signIn;
export const signOut = async () => (await authResult()).signOut;
```

### Cloudflare D1 Adapter — Table Setup

The D1 adapter creates 4 tables: `accounts`, `sessions`, `users`, `verification_tokens`. Run the `up()` migration on first deploy:

```ts
// src/app/api/setup/route.ts (one-time setup endpoint)
import { up } from "@auth/d1-adapter";
import { getCloudflareContext } from "@opennextjs/cloudflare";

export async function GET() {
  const { env } = await getCloudflareContext({ async: true });
  try {
    await up(env.DB);
    return new Response("Auth tables created", { status: 200 });
  } catch (e: any) {
    return new Response(e.message, { status: 500 });
  }
}
```

After running, remove or protect this endpoint.

## Middleware — First Layer of Defense

```ts
// src/middleware.ts
import NextAuth from "next-auth";
import { authConfig } from "./auth.config";

export const { auth: middleware } = NextAuth(authConfig);

export const config = {
  matcher: [
    "/((?!api/auth|_next/static|_next/image|favicon.ico|login|register|public).*)",
  ],
};
```

**CRITICAL:** Middleware is a routing-level gate only. It can be bypassed (e.g., CVE-2025-29927). ALWAYS double-validate with `auth()` inside Server Components, Server Actions, and Route Handlers.

## Defense in Depth — Always Double-Validate

### In Server Components

```ts
// src/app/dashboard/page.tsx
import { auth } from "@/auth";
import { redirect } from "next/navigation";

export default async function DashboardPage() {
  const session = await auth();
  if (!session?.user) redirect("/login");

  return <Dashboard user={session.user} />;
}
```

### In Server Actions

```ts
"use server";

import { auth } from "@/auth";

export async function updateProfile(formData: FormData) {
  const session = await auth();
  if (!session?.user) throw new Error("Unauthorized");

  // ... perform mutation
}
```

### In Route Handlers

```ts
// src/app/api/resumes/route.ts
import { auth } from "@/auth";
import { NextResponse } from "next/server";

export async function GET() {
  const session = await auth();
  if (!session?.user) {
    return NextResponse.json({ error: "Unauthorized" }, { status: 401 });
  }

  // ... return data
}
```

## TypeScript Module Augmentation

Auth.js v5 silently drops custom session fields unless you augment the types AND copy them through callbacks. Both steps are required:

```ts
// src/types/next-auth.d.ts
import type { DefaultSession, DefaultUser } from "next-auth";
import type { DefaultJWT } from "next-auth/jwt";

declare module "next-auth" {
  interface User extends DefaultUser {
    role?: string;
  }
  interface Session {
    user: {
      id: string;
      role: string;
    } & DefaultSession["user"];
  }
}

declare module "next-auth/jwt" {
  interface JWT extends DefaultJWT {
    id?: string;
    role?: string;
  }
}
```

## Role-Based Access Control (RBAC)

### Define Roles and Permissions

```ts
// src/lib/permissions.ts
export type Role = "free" | "pro" | "admin";

const roleHierarchy: Record<Role, number> = {
  free: 1,
  pro: 2,
  admin: 3,
};

export function hasRole(userRole: string | undefined, requiredRole: Role): boolean {
  if (!userRole) return false;
  return (roleHierarchy[userRole as Role] ?? 0) >= roleHierarchy[requiredRole];
}
```

### Require Role in Server Components

```ts
import { auth } from "@/auth";
import { redirect } from "next/navigation";
import { hasRole } from "@/lib/permissions";

export async function requireRole(role: Role) {
  const session = await auth();
  if (!session?.user) redirect("/login");
  if (!hasRole(session.user.role, role)) redirect("/unauthorized");
  return session;
}

// Usage in a page
export default async function ProFeaturePage() {
  const session = await requireRole("pro");
  return <ProFeature user={session.user} />;
}
```

### Feature Gating (Free vs Pro)

```ts
// src/lib/feature-flags.ts
import type { Role } from "./permissions";

const features = {
  aiResumeGeneration: ["pro", "admin"],
  unlimitedResumes: ["pro", "admin"],
  exportPdf: ["free", "pro", "admin"],
  customDomain: ["admin"],
} as const satisfies Record<string, readonly Role[]>;

export type Feature = keyof typeof features;

export function canAccess(userRole: string | undefined, feature: Feature): boolean {
  if (!userRole) return false;
  return (features[feature] as readonly string[]).includes(userRole);
}
```

## Session Strategy — JWT vs Database

| Strategy | Pros | Cons | When to Use |
|---|---|---|---|
| `"jwt"` | No DB lookup per request, edge-compatible, stateless | Cannot revoke instantly, token size grows with claims | Default for Cloudflare/edge deployments |
| `"database"` | Instant revocation, minimal token size | DB query per request, needs edge-compatible adapter | When instant session revocation is critical |

**For Cloudflare D1 deployments, prefer JWT strategy.** Database sessions require a DB query on every request, adding latency. If you need revocation, implement a JWT blocklist in KV.

```ts
session: {
  strategy: "jwt",
  maxAge: 30 * 24 * 60 * 60, // 30 days
},
```

## Provider Configuration Patterns

### OAuth Providers

```ts
import Google from "next-auth/providers/google";
import GitHub from "next-auth/providers/github";

Google({
  clientId: process.env.AUTH_GOOGLE_ID!,
  clientSecret: process.env.AUTH_GOOGLE_SECRET!,
  authorization: {
    params: {
      access_type: "offline",
      prompt: "consent",
    },
  },
}),
```

### Magic Link (Resend)

```ts
import Resend from "next-auth/providers/resend";

Resend({
  apiKey: process.env.AUTH_RESEND_KEY!,
  from: "CVFairy <auth@cvfairy.com>",
}),
```

### Credentials Provider (Use Sparingly)

Credentials provider does NOT persist sessions to the DB adapter and requires manual security handling. Prefer OAuth or magic links:

```ts
import Credentials from "next-auth/providers/credentials";
import { z } from "zod";
import bcrypt from "bcryptjs";

Credentials({
  credentials: {
    email: { label: "Email", type: "email" },
    password: { label: "Password", type: "password" },
  },
  async authorize(credentials) {
    const parsed = z.object({
      email: z.string().email(),
      password: z.string().min(8),
    }).safeParse(credentials);

    if (!parsed.success) return null;

    const user = await db.select().from(users)
      .where(eq(users.email, parsed.data.email))
      .get();

    if (!user?.passwordHash) return null;

    const valid = await bcrypt.compare(parsed.data.password, user.passwordHash);
    if (!valid) return null;

    return { id: user.id, email: user.email, name: user.name, role: user.role };
  },
}),
```

## API Route Handlers

```ts
// src/app/api/auth/[...nextauth]/route.ts
import { handlers } from "@/auth";

const { GET, POST } = await handlers();
export { GET, POST };
```

## Client-Side Session Access

```tsx
// In a Client Component
"use client";
import { useSession } from "next-auth/react";

export function UserMenu() {
  const { data: session, status } = useSession();

  if (status === "loading") return <Skeleton />;
  if (!session) return <LoginButton />;

  return <div>{session.user.name}</div>;
}
```

Wrap the app in `<SessionProvider>`:

```tsx
// src/app/layout.tsx
import { SessionProvider } from "next-auth/react";

export default function RootLayout({ children }: { children: React.ReactNode }) {
  return (
    <html>
      <body>
        <SessionProvider>{children}</SessionProvider>
      </body>
    </html>
  );
}
```

## Environment Variables

```env
# Required
AUTH_SECRET=          # openssl rand -base64 33
AUTH_URL=             # https://cvfairy.com (production URL)

# OAuth — Google
AUTH_GOOGLE_ID=
AUTH_GOOGLE_SECRET=

# OAuth — GitHub
AUTH_GITHUB_ID=
AUTH_GITHUB_SECRET=

# Magic Link — Resend
AUTH_RESEND_KEY=
AUTH_EMAIL_FROM=

# Cloudflare (for drizzle-kit migrations)
CLOUDFLARE_ACCOUNT_ID=
CLOUDFLARE_DATABASE_ID=
CLOUDFLARE_D1_TOKEN=
```

For Cloudflare Workers, set secrets via `wrangler secret put AUTH_SECRET` (not in `wrangler.toml`).

## Edge Runtime Compatibility

### What Works at the Edge

- JWT session strategy
- OAuth providers (Google, GitHub, etc.)
- `auth.config.ts` (no adapter)
- Middleware route protection

### What Does NOT Work at the Edge

- Database adapters (Drizzle, Prisma) — require Node.js runtime
- `bcrypt` — use `bcryptjs` instead
- Node.js `crypto` — use Web Crypto API (`crypto.randomUUID()`, `crypto.subtle`)
- Credentials provider `authorize()` with DB calls — keep in Node.js runtime

### Runtime Declaration

Mark route segments that use the DB adapter as Node.js runtime:

```ts
// src/app/api/auth/[...nextauth]/route.ts
export const runtime = "nodejs"; // NOT "edge" — adapter needs Node.js
```

## Anti-Patterns — NEVER Do These

1. **Never rely on middleware alone for auth.** Always call `auth()` in Server Components, Server Actions, and Route Handlers.
2. **Never expose `user.id` to client-side code unnecessarily.** Only include it in server-side session checks.
3. **Never put role checks only on the client.** Client-side checks are cosmetic. Server enforces.
4. **Never import database adapters in `auth.config.ts`.** It runs at the edge.
5. **Never skip the `jwt()` and `session()` callbacks.** Auth.js v5 silently drops custom fields without them.
6. **Never use `getServerSession()` or `getToken()`.** Those are v4 patterns. Use `auth()` from your `auth.ts`.
7. **Never store secrets in `wrangler.toml`.** Use `wrangler secret put` for AUTH_SECRET and OAuth credentials.
8. **Never use `bcrypt`** in Workers/edge — use `bcryptjs` (pure JS implementation).
9. **Never define inline Server Actions inside Client Components.** Place them in `src/server/actions/` with `"use server"`.
10. **Never forget `trustHost: true`** for deployments behind a proxy (Cloudflare, Vercel).

## File Structure

```
src/
  auth.config.ts       # Edge-safe providers + authorized callback
  auth.ts              # Full config with D1 adapter, jwt/session callbacks
  middleware.ts         # Route-level auth gate
  types/
    next-auth.d.ts     # Module augmentation for Session, User, JWT
  lib/
    permissions.ts     # Role hierarchy + hasRole helper
    feature-flags.ts   # Feature gating (free/pro/admin)
  server/
    actions/           # Server Actions with auth checks
  app/
    api/
      auth/
        [...nextauth]/
          route.ts     # API route handlers
      setup/
        route.ts       # One-time D1 table setup
    login/
      page.tsx         # Sign-in page
    dashboard/
      page.tsx         # Protected page (calls auth())
```

## Callback Data Flow

The data flow is linear and must not be broken:

```
authorize() / OAuth profile
    → jwt({ token, user })     // Copy user.role → token.role on sign-in
    → session({ session, token }) // Copy token.role → session.user.role on every request
    → auth()                   // Returns session with role in Server Components
```

If you skip either callback, `role` will be `undefined` everywhere downstream. Auth.js v5 does NOT warn you — it silently omits the field.
